# The hooks and the installer are POSIX sh and bash. Git Bash on Windows
# happens to tolerate CRLF in them today — the Windows CI job confirmed the
# hooks arrive with CRLF terminators and still work — but that is the
# interpreter being forgiving, not the scripts being correct. A `\r` at a
# line end is part of the last argument to any other sh, which fails with an
# error naming neither the line endings nor the hook.
#
# Pin them to LF so it does not depend on that tolerance.
*.sh            text eol=lf
install.sh      text eol=lf
.githooks/*     text eol=lf
templates/githooks/* text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the files the hooks read and the config they load.
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
